<ol>
    <li>&nbsp;</li>
</ol>

<p>a. \(K_{sp}=[Ag^{+}][I^{-}]\)</p>

<p>b. \(K_{sp}=[Ca^{2+}][F^{-}]^{2}\)</p>

<p>c. \(K_{sp}=[Pb^{2+}][Cl^{-}]^{2}\)</p>

<p>d. \(K_{sp}=[Ag^{+}]^{2}[CrO_{4}^{2-}]\)</p>

<p>2. The solubility constant expression, \(K_{sp}\) is an equilibrium constant for a solid substance dissolving in a an aqueous solution. Thus, it is a measure of solubility and species that do not dissolve are not represented in the solubility product expression.</p>

<p>3. The main difference between \(Q\) and \(K_sp\) is that \(Q\) describes a reaction that is not at equilibrium unlike \(K_{sp}\).</p>

<p>4. An ion product can be used to determine whether a solution is saturated as it is compared to \(K_{sp}\) where there are three possible conditions for an aqueous solution of an ionic solid:</p>

<ol class="mt-indent-1">
    <li>
    <p>\(Q&lt;K_{sp}\). The solution is unsaturated, and more of the ionic solid, if available, will dissolve.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>\(Q=K_{sp}\). The solution is saturated and at equilibrium.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p>\(Q&gt;K_{sp}\). The solution is supersaturated, and ionic solid will precipitate.</p>
    </li>
</ol>

<ol start="5">
    <li>For a 1:1 salt, the molar solubility is simply \(\sqrt{K_{\textrm{sp}}}\); for a 2:1 salt, the molar solubility is \(\sqrt[3]{K_{\textrm{sp}}/4}\). Consequently, the magnitudes of <em>K</em><sub>sp</sub> can be correlated with molar solubility only if the salts have the same stoichiometry.</li>
    <li>By the presence of a common ion, the solubility of any sparingly soluble salt is always decreased. This is similar to the common ion effect found in buffers as adding a common cation or anion shifts the solubility equilibrium in the direction predicted by Le Chatelier&rsquo;s principle.</li>
</ol>

<ol start="7">
    <li>Because of the common ion effect. Adding a soluble Mg<sup>2</sup><sup>+</sup> salt increases [Mg2+] in solution, and Le Chatelier&rsquo;s principle predicts that this will shift the solubility equilibrium of MgCO<sub>3</sub> to the left, decreasing its solubility.</li>
</ol>

